---
title: "Internal investigation rules: mandatory by 16/12/2026"
date: 2026-11-16
lang: en
category: Payroll & HR
excerpt: "Without compliant rules, any HR investigation will be void and evidence inadmissible in court after this deadline."
published: true
author: AgiFid
---

In exactly one month, 
on 16 December 2026
, all Belgian SMEs must have adopted internal rules on private investigations. Without this document, it will be impossible to conduct a valid investigation in cases of fraud, theft or information leakage. And the consequences extend far beyond a mere administrative formality.

## Investigation without rules = evidence nullity


If an investigation concerning an employee is conducted after 16 December 2026 without rules in place, the findings of that investigation may be considered void
. Result: 
evidence gathered cannot be relied upon in disciplinary proceedings, dismissal or any other sanction against an employee
.

In practical terms, if you discover that a colleague is committing serious fraud but you have no compliant rules, you risk being unable to use the evidence of that fraud before the tribunal. 
The findings of the investigation will be void, meaning you cannot use them as evidence for dismissal for serious cause
.

## All SMEs are concerned


The scope of application is particularly broad: any employer intending to conduct an investigation into employees is subject to it, and the size of the company, the number of employees or the frequency of investigations are not relevant
. 


Even an SME without an HR department must establish internal rules in advance if it wishes to conduct or have internal private investigations conducted
. Whether you have 3, 15 or 80 employees, the obligation is identical.

## What do these rules cover?

The rules must specify explicitly and transparently:

- The situations in which investigations may be carried out (fraud, theft, harassment, data leakage...)
- The persons or departments responsible for conducting these investigations
- The authorised investigation methods and their limits
- The arrangements for informing employees


You choose the form yourself: a provision in the work rules, a collective labour agreement or a separate policy
.

## Where does this obligation come from?


The law regulating private investigations came into force on 16 December 2024, with a two-year transitional period during which employers must establish rules
. This law replaces the old 1991 legislation which only concerned private detectives. 

Henceforth, the framework extends to internal investigations conducted by the employer themselves or their HR department. 
A large number of the obligations are prescribed on pain of nullity
, which explains the critical importance of this deadline.

## What to do now?

With one month remaining before the deadline, the urgency is real:

1. **Check** whether your company already has compliant rules
2. **Draft** or adapt the rules according to legal requirements
3. **Consult** the works council or employee representatives
4. **Communicate** the rules to employees (display, file with the Social Laws Inspectorate)

Several HR service providers (Securex, Liantis, Acerta) offer suitable templates. The Institute of Forensic Auditors has also developed a standard set of rules.

**Warning:** the consultation and administrative implementation period can take several weeks. Do not assume you can finalise this in early December.

## What if you are not ready?


Once the deadline has passed, employers who have not put rules in place will no longer be authorised to use private investigators
. 
An employer who engages an external investigation firm will have to prove that they have compliant rules, failing which the firm will have to refuse the assignment
.

The risk is not theoretical: any investigation situation post-16 December will be subject to this strict framework. And in a context where incidents (cybersecurity, internal fraud, conflicts of interest) are becoming more frequent, the absence of rules can quickly become a major legal handicap.

**Key point:** 16 December 2026 is not a target date — it is a firm deadline. If you are not yet compliant, now is the time to act.
